1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:02,400
Underground labyrinths.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,680
Super-sized machines ripped deep into the earth.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,240
Gargantuan scoops lift thousands of tons from massive open pits.

4
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:21,960
High-tech operations extract a resource that accounts for half of our electric power.

5
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:27,520
Now, Coal Mines on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
To reach their work site, miners at the Camp Creek Mine in East Lynn, West Virginia,

7
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:50,280
must travel seven miles through the insides of a mountain.

8
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:57,180
The workers ride a rickety rail cart through a long cavernous tunnel that is their highway.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
After their one hour commute, they finally reach the working face of the mine.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Here, a monstrous machine with foot-long teeth chews away at the earth.

11
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:20,000
This is one of the most efficient underground mining methods ever developed,

12
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:22,000
continuous mining.

13
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:28,000
This technique accounts for almost half of the coal retrieved by underground mining today.

14
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,040
The machine, called a continuous miner, cuts up the seam of coal from the mining face, eliminating the need for blasting or drilling.

15
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,840
It has a large rotating drum-shaped cutting head that spins and breaks the coal from the seam.

16
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,640
We're at the front end of the continuous miner.

17
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:52,320
This is the end of the miner, which actually breaks or extracts the coal from the face.

18
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:57,300
As you can see, there's an 11-and-a-half-foot drum on the front of this miner.

19
00:01:57,301 --> 00:02:02,980
It rotates. It has bits laced on the drum in a scroll fashion.

20
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:11,560
This continuous miner can mine in one minute what took a miner in the 1920s an entire day to produce.

21
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:15,240
These powerful machines are remote-controlled.

22
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:20,120
Today we've got about 250 foot with each miner.

23
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,440
This remote, you can work anything on this miner that you could with sitting in a day.

24
00:02:24,441 --> 00:02:27,940
You can turn the head on, you can raise it up and down, you can turn the conveyor on.

25
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:32,620
Anything that you need control on that miner can be controlled through this box.

26
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:40,000
The machine has moving arms that scoop the coal directly onto a conveyor that leads to a shuttle car.

27
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:45,860
A miner then drives the shuttle car to a longer conveyor that carries the coal to the surface.

28
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:50,220
A continuous miner is going to have different productivity rates,

29
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:55,860
but typically 6,000 raw tons a day would be a reasonable expectation.

30
00:02:57,740 --> 00:03:01,840
200 miles away in the Bailey Mine in Wayne County, Pennsylvania,

31
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:05,100
an even more impressive machine is hard at work.

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:06,800
The longwall.

33
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,580
Longwall mining is another approach used in underground mines.

34
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:15,000
Perhaps the most productive current underground mining method.

35
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:22,860
Unlike the continuous miner, the enormous longwall cuts across one very long seam of coal

36
00:03:22,861 --> 00:03:27,860
that is broken apart by a rotating drum that sweeps back and forth.

37
00:03:27,861 --> 00:03:31,860
The coal falls directly onto a built-in conveyor.

38
00:03:31,861 --> 00:03:36,860
This technique can recover up to 40,000 tons of coal per day.

39
00:03:36,861 --> 00:03:40,860
Longwall machines have revolutionized underground coal mining.

40
00:03:40,861 --> 00:03:47,440
The longwall machine has a series of cutting heads or shears that chew away at the coal.

41
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:50,760
They got cutting bits on it and they break the coal.

42
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:52,120
It falls onto a belt line.

43
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:54,620
That entire coal seam is removed.

44
00:03:54,621 --> 00:03:57,800
And these panels are about a thousand feet wide on the average.

45
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,040
And they will sometimes run as much as a mile.

46
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:02,240
I call it Star Wars technology.

47
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:06,280
Early versions of the longwalls were developed in the late 1950s.

48
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,260
Today, the machine is completely automated.

49
00:04:09,261 --> 00:04:15,300
It has a hydraulically operated steel canopy that keeps the roof of the mine secure

50
00:04:15,301 --> 00:04:19,140
and moves along with the machine as the coal seam is cut.

51
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:23,520
The roof in the mined out areas collapses in a controlled manner.

52
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:31,460
If you can imagine the early days where you were extracting the coal using a pick,

53
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:37,700
using a shovel to load it into a wagon that was pulled outside by a pony,

54
00:04:37,701 --> 00:04:42,680
is significantly different than using a remote control with a joystick

55
00:04:42,681 --> 00:04:45,820
to operate a 600-horsepower continuous miner.

56
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:52,100
Today's underground mines look much different than they did years ago.

57
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:55,840
The Camp Creek Shaft Mine in West Virginia is no exception.

58
00:04:57,380 --> 00:05:02,160
The walls of the tunnels of this deep coal mine are surprisingly white instead of black.

59
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:06,260
This is due to the application of rock dust,

60
00:05:06,261 --> 00:05:12,640
which is ground-up lime that is sprayed to the mine roof, floor, and ribs to help prevent mine explosions.

61
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:19,120
Limestone dilutes any hazardous concentrations of coal dust, which can cause explosions.

62
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:26,180
Large ventilating fans also remove any lingering coal dust, as well as methane gas.

63
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:33,060
On the surface, huge ventilating fans are used to cause a pressure differential

64
00:05:33,061 --> 00:05:36,740
to pull atmospheric air from the outside into the coal mines.

65
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:40,060
Ventilation does primarily two things.

66
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:47,220
Removes gases from the mining areas and helps to control and reduce dust.

67
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:54,100
As you can see here, a ventilating curtain is used to direct air in the working faces

68
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:56,920
to where the employees are performing their duties.

69
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:02,800
Coal deposits exist on every continent, including Antarctica.

70
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:09,060
It is the fuel most widely used to produce steam to drive the turbines of power plant generators.

71
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:19,780
When coal is burned, the carbon and hydrogen naturally found in the fuel combine with oxygen to produce carbon dioxide and water vapor.

72
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:22,100
This reaction gives off heat energy.

73
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:31,460
About 55 to 56 percent of the electricity that people use in this country is made by burning coal.

74
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:37,780
The electric utility companies use more than 80% of all coal mined in the U.S.

75
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:44,560
The concrete, paper, and glass industries also consume large quantities of coal

76
00:06:44,561 --> 00:06:47,900
as a fuel for heating and powering their facilities.

77
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:52,500
The steel industry is reliant on coal to produce coke,

78
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:56,900
a hard carbon material used in the manufacture of iron and steel.

79
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,880
The carbon in coal gives steel its strength and versatility.

80
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:14,161
For railroad tracks, locomotives, automobiles, steamships, the girders and skyscrapers, steel and the coal that was used to heat the iron to make it are the essential

81
00:07:14,162 --> 00:07:16,301
Steel is the sinew of modernity.

82
00:07:16,302 --> 00:07:22,520
ingredients, an abundant source of economical power to create the modern world as we know it.

83
00:07:22,521 --> 00:07:35,725
These impressive technological advances are the culmination of a coal mining effort that reaches as far back as 3,000 years for a resource that was formed millions of

84
00:07:35,726 --> 00:07:36,520
years ago.

85
00:07:36,521 --> 00:07:47,520
Affectionately referred to as buried sunshine, coal is a fossil fuel formed from vegetation that grew in swamps as long as 400 million years ago.

86
00:07:47,521 --> 00:07:51,620
This vegetation absorbed and captured the sun's energy

87
00:07:51,621 --> 00:07:57,220
and sank to the bottom of marsh areas to decompose into a soggy dense material

88
00:07:57,221 --> 00:08:01,520
called peat. As the surface of the earth changed,

89
00:08:01,521 --> 00:08:05,820
geological forces buried the peat under heavy layers of rock.

90
00:08:05,821 --> 00:08:09,120
The continuing pressure and heat

91
00:08:09,121 --> 00:08:13,820
transformed the compressed peat into coal. When the coal is uncovered,

92
00:08:13,821 --> 00:08:18,340
It is located in strata, or seams like the filling in a multi-layered cake.

93
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:23,440
Early civilizations found ways to mine this buried fuel

94
00:08:23,441 --> 00:08:26,640
long before it became an ample source of electric power.

95
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:32,260
The first records we have of coal being used, and therefore presumably mined,

96
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:35,220
date back about 3,000 years ago.

97
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:38,540
Coal was used for funeral piles in Wales.

98
00:08:38,541 --> 00:08:41,840
The Chinese probably used it around the same period.

99
00:08:41,841 --> 00:08:46,000
and Aristotle records the rock that burned.

100
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:51,060
So we think that somewhere between probably 3,000 to 2,000 years ago,

101
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:52,000
coal mining started.

102
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:54,560
We have no record of how it was mined at that point,

103
00:08:54,561 --> 00:08:57,500
but we assume that they simply mined the outcrops of coal

104
00:08:57,501 --> 00:08:59,780
where it appeared on the side of a hill or a cliff.

105
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:06,540
Before 400 A.D., coal was burned and used as a source of heat

106
00:09:06,541 --> 00:09:08,300
during the Roman occupation of Britain.

107
00:09:08,301 --> 00:09:14,320
and it was being used in America as early as 1000 AD by the Hopi Indians

108
00:09:14,321 --> 00:09:16,640
in what is now Arizona.

109
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:21,980
They also used coal for heating purposes and to bake their pottery.

110
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:25,940
Scholars believe that laborers and peasants both in England and America

111
00:09:25,941 --> 00:09:28,680
dug small surface pits for domestic fuel.

112
00:09:29,300 --> 00:09:33,600
But for the most part, wood was still the main source of fuel.

113
00:09:33,601 --> 00:09:41,520
The first recorded mining of coal that we have written record of refers to sea coals.

114
00:09:41,521 --> 00:09:49,620
Sea coals occurred along the beaches of Scotland and northeastern England, and it was simply

115
00:09:49,621 --> 00:09:50,620
picked up.

116
00:09:50,621 --> 00:09:56,280
Where coal seams basically occurred on the cliffs, as the coast eroded away, the coal

117
00:09:56,281 --> 00:09:58,720
would have appeared on the beaches like any other rock.

118
00:09:58,721 --> 00:10:05,920
It's apparently by the 13th century that sea coals were the first coals actually taken down to the big cities,

119
00:10:06,220 --> 00:10:08,900
London being the major one, as a source of fuel.

120
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:14,680
London was starting to run out of firewood, so this sea coal became a popular replacement.

121
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:19,160
The increased need for coal in other parts of Europe during the same period

122
00:10:19,161 --> 00:10:24,120
was a direct result of the diminishing supply of forests and wood as fuel.

123
00:10:24,121 --> 00:10:31,160
Great emphasis was placed on the development of the coal fields due to the economics of the time

124
00:10:31,161 --> 00:10:36,440
Coal simply became a cheaper fuel source than wood at that point

125
00:10:36,441 --> 00:10:43,700
Miners would now have to dig to provide an adequate supply of coal to keep up with the new demand

126
00:10:43,701 --> 00:10:50,760
Next, with new methods of retrieval, coal becomes the main source of fuel in the world

127
00:10:50,761 --> 00:10:53,920
But not without a price

128
00:10:54,120 --> 00:11:04,760
Each day, the average American uses electricity equal to the amount of energy found in 21 pounds of coal.

129
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,700
Coal Mines will return on Modern Marvels.

130
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:15,920
We now return to Coal Mines on Modern Marvels.

131
00:11:15,921 --> 00:11:24,600
As the forests of Europe began to dwindle, coal emerged as the only other source of fuel.

132
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:29,220
Early miners needed to find new ways of extracting it.

133
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:34,680
The bell pit method of coal mining evolved as early as the 14th century,

134
00:11:35,460 --> 00:11:39,100
when monasteries and castles used coal for domestic purposes.

135
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:44,140
The name comes from the final shape of the excavation.

136
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:48,440
miners dug vertical shafts straight down through the coal seam

137
00:11:48,441 --> 00:11:50,400
where they created a bell-shaped chamber

138
00:11:50,401 --> 00:11:54,440
that allowed them to extract as much coal as possible

139
00:11:54,441 --> 00:11:56,400
without the roof caving in.

140
00:11:57,980 --> 00:12:01,060
These narrow shafts were no more than 30 feet deep

141
00:12:01,061 --> 00:12:02,620
and presented few dangers.

142
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:05,540
Explosives were not in use yet,

143
00:12:05,541 --> 00:12:07,820
so the miners broke through rock and coal

144
00:12:07,821 --> 00:12:09,820
with nothing more than primitive tools,

145
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:13,600
such as stone hammers, picks, and shovels.

146
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:18,680
Coal mining originally was done by hand, pick and shovel.

147
00:12:19,380 --> 00:12:21,760
The coal was hacked out of the coal seam,

148
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:25,860
put in baskets where small children or women could haul it to the surface.

149
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:32,180
In a deep mine, a shaft with a winch crank at the top could lift buckets of coal out.

150
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:37,020
Ladders were used for access, and candles were the only source of light.

151
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:41,420
The coal was sorted into different sizes, usually by the women and children.

152
00:12:41,421 --> 00:12:46,420
This mining method continued to be used for centuries.

153
00:12:46,421 --> 00:12:53,420
Drift mines evolved as another method of coal mining, starting in the 15th century.

154
00:12:53,421 --> 00:13:01,420
Drift mining would have occurred where a coal seam disappeared into a cliff or a steep side of a hill,

155
00:13:01,421 --> 00:13:06,420
where putting in bell pits would have required the development of enormous shafts.

156
00:13:06,421 --> 00:13:13,200
and so the only way to mine the coal beyond the outcrop was simply to drive a tunnel in and follow

157
00:13:13,201 --> 00:13:18,720
the coal seam and this would have been the start of drift mining drift being the mining word for

158
00:13:18,721 --> 00:13:25,680
tunnel the miners who drift mind again rapidly learn the basic rules of room and pillar mining

159
00:13:25,681 --> 00:13:31,500
that is you can't make an opening too wide or the roof will collapse on you and you have to leave

160
00:13:31,501 --> 00:13:34,020
pillars of coal behind you to support the roof.

161
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:39,420
The most common form of injury and death was a roof fall in a coal mine.

162
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:44,680
The deeper that a mine went underground, the more difficult it was to keep the top up,

163
00:13:44,980 --> 00:13:49,300
to keep the mine safe so that miners could get in and coal could come out.

164
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:55,700
The simplest way to do that was to cut mine timbers properly set in place with shims at

165
00:13:55,701 --> 00:13:57,760
the top and bottom to ensure a tight fit.

166
00:13:57,761 --> 00:14:02,580
That was the most common form of keeping the top off of the coal miner in the coal mine.

167
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:08,200
These coal mining methods remained relatively unchanged for several hundred years.

168
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:14,420
It wasn't until the 17th century that England began to advance mining techniques.

169
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:22,320
Up until then, miners hauled coal and wicker baskets on their backs, then in wheelbarrows or on sleds.

170
00:14:22,321 --> 00:14:25,840
by the mid-17th century

171
00:14:25,841 --> 00:14:28,180
animals began to be used

172
00:14:28,181 --> 00:14:30,120
to pull coal carts in the English mines

173
00:14:30,121 --> 00:14:32,840
England also introduced

174
00:14:32,841 --> 00:14:34,240
mine railway systems

175
00:14:34,241 --> 00:14:36,280
as well as a reliable system

176
00:14:36,281 --> 00:14:37,160
to drain water

177
00:14:37,161 --> 00:14:39,920
and prevent flooding in the deeper mines

178
00:14:39,921 --> 00:14:42,540
as they mined out the near surface

179
00:14:42,541 --> 00:14:43,920
coal seams and they went deeper

180
00:14:43,921 --> 00:14:46,520
they started to encounter large quantities of water

181
00:14:46,521 --> 00:14:48,540
and the systems that they had

182
00:14:48,541 --> 00:14:50,020
of pumping water were so primitive

183
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:55,360
What they really needed to solve the water problem was a source of power, a pumping system.

184
00:14:55,780 --> 00:14:59,780
Up until the 18th century, water wheels were the only real source of power.

185
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:06,080
In 1774, James Watt perfected the steam engine,

186
00:15:06,580 --> 00:15:10,960
which finally resolved the problem of efficiently pumping water out of the deeper mines.

187
00:15:13,020 --> 00:15:19,300
Although the American Indians had used coal found in small surface pits as early as 1000 A.D.,

188
00:15:19,301 --> 00:15:26,300
It wasn't until the Revolutionary War that more sophisticated coal mining operations appeared.

189
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:36,300
Coal mined from small drift mines in Richmond, Virginia, was used in the manufacture of weapons for the American troops.

190
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:41,300
Before the war, most of the coal came from England.

191
00:15:42,300 --> 00:15:48,300
But even after the American Revolution, Britain continued to provide America with coal through the early 1800s.

192
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:55,780
Coal was basically imported to the United States up until the War of 1812.

193
00:15:56,580 --> 00:16:00,780
In fact, Britain really discouraged the mining of coal in the United States.

194
00:16:00,781 --> 00:16:04,300
They didn't want anybody competing basically with Britain economically.

195
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:09,680
And so what we have is we have this curious condition of coal being literally shipped across the Atlantic

196
00:16:09,681 --> 00:16:14,700
when there were enormous quantities known to exist here in the United States.

197
00:16:14,701 --> 00:16:24,220
But after the War of 1812, it took very little time before people started to mine coal here in the U.S., even though there was enormous quantities of wood.

198
00:16:26,280 --> 00:16:29,956
The first record of continuous coal mining in the U.S.

199
00:16:29,957 --> 00:16:33,700
goes back to 1840 in the Richmond, Virginia coal basin.

200
00:16:35,860 --> 00:16:41,520
The Americans used the British innovation of huge steam pumps to drain water from the deeper mines.

201
00:16:41,521 --> 00:16:47,220
however the other problem was the need to safely ventilate the mines and push

202
00:16:47,221 --> 00:16:52,800
methane out pockets of methane gas formed millions of years ago are trapped

203
00:16:52,801 --> 00:16:57,600
in coal seams and are extremely flammable when mixed with air and often

204
00:16:57,601 --> 00:17:03,900
lead to deadly explosions until the introduction of steam and later electric

205
00:17:03,901 --> 00:17:09,020
fans miners ventilated underground mines by creating furnaces at the bottom of

206
00:17:09,021 --> 00:17:09,721
shaft.

207
00:17:09,722 --> 00:17:16,420
The first working fan in an underground coal mine probably occurred in Belgium in about

208
00:17:16,421 --> 00:17:17,121
1835.

209
00:17:17,122 --> 00:17:24,480
They were powered by steam engines, not very effective, but better than the alternative,

210
00:17:24,481 --> 00:17:30,042
which was typically the use of fires to ventilate coal mines.

211
00:17:30,043 --> 00:17:32,960
The idea is simply that the fire

212
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:38,220
heats the air, and just as a chimney in a house draws the air through the mine.

213
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:42,560
If it's a gassy coal mine, when the methane gas hits that fire,

214
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:45,100
as it will do in pockets on occasions,

215
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:47,500
you can have an incredibly disastrous explosion.

216
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:52,320
Another problem the miners encountered underground was oxygen deficiency,

217
00:17:52,980 --> 00:17:54,720
also known as black damp.

218
00:17:55,400 --> 00:17:56,920
Enter the legendary canary.

219
00:17:58,060 --> 00:18:02,940
The cage was actually used to carry a canary in a coal mine,

220
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:06,440
and the canary was there for what they call black damp.

221
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:12,620
They found a long time ago that the canary would die in low oxygen before a person would.

222
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:16,860
Miners would trim the canary's claws because they discovered

223
00:18:16,861 --> 00:18:19,320
that the bird could die from the lack of oxygen,

224
00:18:19,660 --> 00:18:23,880
but still grip its perch and appear to be alive due to the effects of rigor mortis.

225
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:31,120
By the late 1800s, coal mining in America was one of the fastest growing industries.

226
00:18:31,121 --> 00:18:34,260
doubling every eight years.

227
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:40,880
Most of the mine operators owned everything else in the small, isolated mining towns,

228
00:18:41,180 --> 00:18:43,000
including the miners' home,

229
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:47,280
leaving the workers powerless and vulnerable to unsafe conditions.

230
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:53,060
Roof falls in coal mines, underground explosions, fires.

231
00:18:53,440 --> 00:18:57,520
Coal mining was the most hazardous occupation in industrialized societies.

232
00:18:57,521 --> 00:19:04,780
The industry was slow to adopt a safety underground, believing that it would limit production and limit employment.

233
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:12,520
Large mining disasters followed the rapid increase in production.

234
00:19:13,780 --> 00:19:18,440
The European mines had already started to experience methane explosions with increasing frequency,

235
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:23,260
and with more tragic results, as the number of miners underground grew.

236
00:19:24,060 --> 00:19:27,660
And as the mines got deeper, the methane content increased.

237
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:32,000
Disaster struck American mines beginning in the 19th century.

238
00:19:32,420 --> 00:19:38,720
But the worst mine explosion in the United States occurred at Monongo, West Virginia in 1907.

239
00:19:39,340 --> 00:19:47,360
As many as 425 miners died when dynamite was improperly handled and a blast ignited the methane and coal dust.

240
00:19:47,980 --> 00:19:56,440
The enormous number of fatalities that were occurring in mines due to explosions and roof falls and carbon monoxide and various other accidents,

241
00:19:57,140 --> 00:19:58,720
we had as a result of that.

242
00:19:59,220 --> 00:20:03,500
In 1910, the U.S. government formed the United States Bureau of Mines.

243
00:20:04,200 --> 00:20:12,940
Its job was simply to do research and come up with improved technologies to greatly enhance the health and safety of mining at that particular time.

244
00:20:12,941 --> 00:20:17,940
Flame safety lamps replaced the archaic canary in a cage.

245
00:20:17,941 --> 00:20:21,940
The flame emits an orange glow if methane is present in the mine.

246
00:20:21,941 --> 00:20:25,940
And if oxygen levels are low, the flame goes out.

247
00:20:25,941 --> 00:20:31,940
Separate ventilation shafts with powerful fans circulating air

248
00:20:31,941 --> 00:20:34,940
replaced the dangerous ventilating furnaces.

249
00:20:34,941 --> 00:20:38,940
Coal dust was linked to black lung disease,

250
00:20:38,941 --> 00:20:41,940
also known as coal miner's pneumoconiosis,

251
00:20:41,941 --> 00:20:47,840
a progressive pulmonary disorder that builds up over years of inhaling high levels of airborne dust particles.

252
00:20:50,180 --> 00:20:57,760
Levels of coal mine dust began to be constantly monitored and controlled by wetting coal or by spraying limestone on the walls.

253
00:20:59,160 --> 00:21:03,440
These improvements had a great impact on reducing injuries and fatalities underground.

254
00:21:05,160 --> 00:21:08,820
Coal mining is not even among the ten most dangerous occupations. Mining is not.

255
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:11,640
Coal mining today is very, very tightly regulated.

256
00:21:11,940 --> 00:21:18,980
But it took the cooperation of the industry, labor, and federal and state regulatory agencies to achieve that record.

257
00:21:20,060 --> 00:21:25,000
Next, incredible advances in coal mining equipment lead to greater efficiency.

258
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:34,880
Coal deposits are found in 38 states, covering 13% of the United States.

259
00:21:36,500 --> 00:21:39,700
Coal Mines will return on Modern Marvels.

260
00:21:41,940 --> 00:21:45,080
We now return to Coal Mines on Modern Marvels.

261
00:21:47,560 --> 00:21:51,020
With the advent of electricity in the latter part of the 19th century,

262
00:21:51,960 --> 00:21:55,120
the mechanization of coal mining began on a large scale.

263
00:21:57,200 --> 00:21:59,420
First, electric hammers and saws.

264
00:21:59,880 --> 00:22:03,020
Then, coal cutting machines began to appear in the underground mines.

265
00:22:03,920 --> 00:22:06,760
But one man in particular would rise above the others

266
00:22:06,761 --> 00:22:09,260
by developing a series of new inventions

267
00:22:09,261 --> 00:22:11,920
that helped revolutionize underground coal mining.

268
00:22:12,700 --> 00:22:17,360
At the age of 12, Joseph Francis Joy followed in his father's footsteps

269
00:22:17,361 --> 00:22:20,220
and went to work in a coal mine in 1895.

270
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:24,020
Frustrated with how hard and dangerous the work was,

271
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:27,020
he became determined to find a better way.

272
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:31,840
By the time he was 36 years old, and after many attempts,

273
00:22:31,841 --> 00:22:35,820
he invented the first successful mechanical coal loading device.

274
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:41,740
In 1919, we have the first of the mechanical loading systems

275
00:22:41,741 --> 00:22:45,520
that was really very, very effective, so effective that it's still widely used today,

276
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:48,440
which is the Joy Gathering Arm Loader.

277
00:22:49,180 --> 00:22:55,280
And this, again, was to really revolutionize mining at that point in history.

278
00:22:55,760 --> 00:22:58,440
These loaders would typically load into coal cars.

279
00:22:58,760 --> 00:23:02,400
The Joy Loader was electrically powered and incredibly effective.

280
00:23:02,920 --> 00:23:09,640
It wasn't until the 1930s that, again, Joy came along and built the first of the shuttle cars.

281
00:23:11,740 --> 00:23:15,240
Joy would go on to accumulate 190 patents in his lifetime,

282
00:23:15,940 --> 00:23:18,920
many of which drastically improved underground mining mechanization.

283
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:24,380
One of the most important was an early version of the continuous miner.

284
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:30,980
In 1947, what we have is, again, Joy comes up with a machine,

285
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:34,360
the first of the continuous miners, the Ripper machine.

286
00:23:35,220 --> 00:23:39,000
The Ripper machine basically tore up the coal.

287
00:23:39,580 --> 00:23:41,040
No longer did you have to undercut it,

288
00:23:41,041 --> 00:23:42,240
How long did you have to drill it?

289
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:43,700
How long did you have to shoot it?

290
00:23:43,920 --> 00:23:49,020
What you now had is one machine that literally tore the coal down onto a gathering arm loader,

291
00:23:49,021 --> 00:23:54,660
and the loader simply then loaded the coal out directly over the back of the machine and into the shuttle guards.

292
00:23:56,820 --> 00:24:03,440
Over the years, Joy and other engineering pioneers reconfigured crude and ineffective machines

293
00:24:03,441 --> 00:24:07,700
that would lead to today's highly efficient and productive underground mines.

294
00:24:07,701 --> 00:24:14,700
After the introduction of the shuttle car, roof bolting systems were developed in the 1940s.

295
00:24:14,701 --> 00:24:19,700
Roof bolts help to prevent cave-ins in the underground mines.

296
00:24:19,701 --> 00:24:24,700
Miners secure the roof over the mined-out area by using a roof bolting machine.

297
00:24:27,700 --> 00:24:35,700
These bolts are long rods driven into the roof to bind several layers of weak strata into another layer that is strong enough to support its own weight.

298
00:24:35,701 --> 00:24:43,200
If I took that coal miner that had been asleep for 40 years into a mine today,

299
00:24:43,380 --> 00:24:52,700
I think he would be wide-eyed and absolutely amazed at the mechanization that has occurred in the mining environment.

300
00:24:53,580 --> 00:24:56,460
As the machinery evolved, so did the miner.

301
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:01,540
Today's miner must have the proper attire before going underground.

302
00:25:02,020 --> 00:25:05,260
This includes a hard hat with battery-operated headlamp.

303
00:25:05,700 --> 00:25:09,500
which replaced the open flame carbide lamp worn by the early miners.

304
00:25:11,660 --> 00:25:14,920
A nickel's worth of carbide in 1900 would last him about a week.

305
00:25:15,180 --> 00:25:17,580
You're going to put enough in there for about two hours.

306
00:25:18,440 --> 00:25:20,080
And so he has to have moisture.

307
00:25:20,380 --> 00:25:23,140
If he didn't have any water with him, what he would do is spit in it.

308
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:27,140
And you see the settling gas is what that's making.

309
00:25:27,141 --> 00:25:30,100
Now, this thing is similar to a cigarette lighter,

310
00:25:30,260 --> 00:25:35,120
but the only way you can make it burn is put your hand there and let the gas build up.

311
00:25:35,121 --> 00:25:40,360
after you left the gas build up, then you hit it with your hand to make the flame.

312
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:47,460
What he used back then was a cloth hat, and so this was kind of hard on his head,

313
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:49,300
especially if he didn't look what was above him.

314
00:25:49,920 --> 00:25:54,640
Safety glasses are an integral part of our safety protection equipment,

315
00:25:55,140 --> 00:25:59,780
obviously to protect the eyes from dust or while using hand tools

316
00:25:59,781 --> 00:26:04,460
and that sort of thing where flying debris could present an eye hazard.

317
00:26:04,461 --> 00:26:08,300
Eye protection was not a consideration in the early days.

318
00:26:08,760 --> 00:26:12,820
Other items not available to the early miner include steel-toed boots,

319
00:26:13,400 --> 00:26:17,300
a portable methane monitor, and an apparatus called a self-rescuer.

320
00:26:18,160 --> 00:26:26,360
A self-rescuer is a device by which a human can breathe in very low oxygen content atmospheres,

321
00:26:26,600 --> 00:26:33,080
and it will provide you the ability to breathe for a period of approximately one hour.

322
00:26:34,460 --> 00:26:39,180
While there have been many changes in the way coal is mined, one thing has remained constant.

323
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:41,840
The types of underground mines.

324
00:26:42,920 --> 00:26:47,280
Drift mines have a tunnel driven horizontally into the side of a hill to reach the coal.

325
00:26:50,520 --> 00:26:54,640
Slope mines are constructed when the bed of coal is relatively close to the surface,

326
00:26:55,360 --> 00:26:57,860
but is too deep to be recovered by surface mining.

327
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:02,040
Shaft mines are the most common and generally the deepest.

328
00:27:02,041 --> 00:27:04,320
some as deep as 3,000 feet.

329
00:27:05,220 --> 00:27:08,460
Elevators carry workers and equipment in and out of this type of mine.

330
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:15,840
A flat vehicle called a man-trip takes miners where they need to go once inside these mines.

331
00:27:16,700 --> 00:27:21,540
And mine cars transport the mining machines in sections that are then assembled underground.

332
00:27:23,320 --> 00:27:29,360
Large automated machines and computerized controls have not only led to greater productivity,

333
00:27:29,361 --> 00:27:37,360
But they have also led to better health and safety for the miners, physically removing them from potentially hazardous situations.

334
00:27:39,520 --> 00:27:47,180
Although mining disasters have decreased dramatically in the last century, working in an underground environment will always have inherent dangers.

335
00:27:48,380 --> 00:27:58,880
In the summer of 2002, the entire country watched as nine miners in Somerset, Pennsylvania, were rescued after being trapped in a flooded shaft mine for three days.

336
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:06,300
The miners tapped into an old, abandoned mine that contained more than 50 million gallons of water that broke through.

337
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,140
But these miners were the fortunate ones.

338
00:28:10,200 --> 00:28:17,520
A year earlier in Alabama, 13 miners died when part of the mine roof collapsed and caused a methane gas explosion.

339
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:22,980
And there have been more tragic recent disasters in underground mines throughout the world.

340
00:28:23,980 --> 00:28:28,160
Next, while underground mining is still a vital aspect of coal mining,

341
00:28:28,580 --> 00:28:33,680
in the 1970s, technological advances would lead to new surface mining methods

342
00:28:33,681 --> 00:28:36,540
that would forever change the way coal is mined.

343
00:28:39,240 --> 00:28:43,460
Underground mining accounts for 35% of the coal mined in America.

344
00:28:45,200 --> 00:28:47,300
Coal Mines will return on Modern Marvels.

345
00:28:47,301 --> 00:28:54,300
We now return to Coal Mines on Modern Marvels.

346
00:28:57,300 --> 00:29:03,040
Nestled among the desolate plains and towering mountain ranges of Wyoming is the North Antelope

347
00:29:03,041 --> 00:29:11,240
Rochelle Coal Mine in the Powder River Basin, the largest coal mine in the world.

348
00:29:11,241 --> 00:29:17,080
This surface mine produces more than 75 million tons of coal a year.

349
00:29:17,081 --> 00:29:20,960
Surface mines are extensively engineered and highly mechanized operations,

350
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:24,660
accounting for 65% of coal produced in the United States.

351
00:29:25,520 --> 00:29:27,940
Most of the coal found in these mines is recovered,

352
00:29:29,080 --> 00:29:32,480
making surface mines twice as productive as underground mines.

353
00:29:33,560 --> 00:29:36,860
What we have now is the development of a mining system, basically,

354
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:40,660
which is incredibly cheap and incredibly productive.

355
00:29:41,180 --> 00:29:46,200
The idea that you simply peel away the surface of the earth to expose the coal seam.

356
00:29:47,080 --> 00:29:54,080
Surface mines didn't appear until the late 1800s in Danville, Illinois.

357
00:29:54,081 --> 00:29:58,080
The great technological development in surface mining goes back to 1911

358
00:29:58,081 --> 00:30:04,080
with the introduction of the Marion Shovel, the first real stripping shovel.

359
00:30:04,081 --> 00:30:08,080
Before World War II, surface mining wasn't the predominant method of mining

360
00:30:08,081 --> 00:30:13,080
because equipment wasn't big enough to move the massive amounts of overburden

361
00:30:13,081 --> 00:30:19,160
burden that to have to be moved in a surface mine to get to the coal scene.

362
00:30:19,161 --> 00:30:23,580
After the war, manufacturing processes led to the development of larger and more productive

363
00:30:23,581 --> 00:30:26,001
surface mining equipment.

364
00:30:26,002 --> 00:30:32,200
And by the 70s, the size of the machines increased dramatically.

365
00:30:32,201 --> 00:30:37,300
Surface mines also tend to be very safe, because most of the work takes place above ground

366
00:30:37,301 --> 00:30:41,740
in large open areas, eliminating methane explosions and cave-ins.

367
00:30:43,400 --> 00:30:48,820
The coal is uncovered by giant earth-moving machines that strip off the overburden, the

368
00:30:48,821 --> 00:30:50,300
earth and rock covering the coal.

369
00:30:51,900 --> 00:30:57,740
The dragline has an enormous bucket suspended from the end of a boom, which extends more

370
00:30:57,741 --> 00:30:59,480
than 275 feet.

371
00:30:59,481 --> 00:31:04,040
draglines are considered the world's largest land-based machines

372
00:31:04,041 --> 00:31:08,320
some of which have 250 ton buckets

373
00:31:08,321 --> 00:31:12,600
that are large enough to park two greyhound buses side by side

374
00:31:12,601 --> 00:31:21,800
this dragline removes approximately 75,000 tons of overburden each day

375
00:31:21,801 --> 00:31:25,920
it will take about a month for that machine to mine

376
00:31:25,921 --> 00:31:30,840
from where he is currently to the far wall on the north end of the pit.

377
00:31:32,120 --> 00:31:38,960
In most surface mines today, the overburden can also be blasted and broken apart by explosives placed in boreholes.

378
00:31:40,200 --> 00:31:43,540
Three, two, one, fire and hold.

379
00:31:43,541 --> 00:31:56,480
We use about 45,000 tons of explosives a year to blast about 240 million cubic yards of overburden.

380
00:31:56,800 --> 00:32:04,360
When the overburden is shot, we bring in either our major drag line stripping equipment or truck shovels.

381
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:08,220
The size of the equipment has increased.

382
00:32:08,660 --> 00:32:13,260
Where we had a 100-ton truck, we have trucks now up to 400 tons.

383
00:32:13,540 --> 00:32:14,740
to haul coal.

384
00:32:17,400 --> 00:32:20,000
After the overburden is blasted and removed,

385
00:32:20,540 --> 00:32:22,980
the exposed coal is then loaded into trucks

386
00:32:22,981 --> 00:32:26,520
by electric power shovels that are 20 stories high.

387
00:32:27,900 --> 00:32:30,040
Huge trucks haul tons of coal,

388
00:32:30,560 --> 00:32:33,860
and covered conveyor systems deliver the newly acquired coal

389
00:32:33,861 --> 00:32:35,480
to nearby preparation plants.

390
00:32:37,440 --> 00:32:40,920
The cut in the mine is then refilled with a reserved overburden.

391
00:32:43,540 --> 00:32:50,280
The site is graded, covered with topsoil, and reseeded so that new vegetation can grow.

392
00:32:52,180 --> 00:32:55,660
Mining companies weren't initially required by law to reclaim the land

393
00:32:55,661 --> 00:33:00,880
until the 1977 Surface Mine Coal Reclamation Act.

394
00:33:02,500 --> 00:33:07,060
What they did is they blast the rock above the seam, push it over the side of the mountain,

395
00:33:07,460 --> 00:33:13,220
expose the coal seam, remove the coal, and then just leave the whole lot behind them as an environmental disaster.

396
00:33:13,540 --> 00:33:20,500
With the passage of the 1977 Surface Mine Coal Reclamation Act, this was no longer allowed.

397
00:33:21,460 --> 00:33:26,080
And through a system of making the mining companies put up huge bonds, basically,

398
00:33:26,440 --> 00:33:31,360
they forced the mining companies to reclaim the land to original condition.

399
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:39,100
Surface mining, also called strip mining, has remained environmentally controversial despite the Reclamation Act.

400
00:33:39,101 --> 00:33:47,800
Strip mining, particularly the way that it's being practiced in parts of West Virginia and Kentucky today with mountaintop removal,

401
00:33:48,080 --> 00:33:59,440
is literally blasting the tops off of mountains and dumping tons of rock and dirt into streams and valleys where miles and miles of streams are being obliterated.

402
00:33:59,920 --> 00:34:06,700
The government is not doing an adequate job of enforcing the environmental laws that are designed to protect the mountains and the valleys.

403
00:34:06,701 --> 00:34:09,700
The SMACRA Act is not being adequately enforced.

404
00:34:10,700 --> 00:34:17,700
The mining companies claim that removing mountaintops and filling in valleys is the only economical way to get to the cleaner burning coal.

405
00:34:18,700 --> 00:34:21,700
While the government is eager to help the companies,

406
00:34:22,700 --> 00:34:26,700
environmentalists and residents living near the strip mines continue to fight them.

407
00:34:27,700 --> 00:34:31,700
In the meantime, new coal mines are still being developed.

408
00:34:32,700 --> 00:34:35,700
Years of planning are required before any extraction can take place.

409
00:34:36,700 --> 00:34:49,180
Once you acquire a lease, then it might take up to 8 to 10 years just through the permitting process with the various state and federal agencies.

410
00:34:50,260 --> 00:35:00,820
For both surface and underground mines, aerial and satellite photography, as well as radar and global positioning systems, are used in locating the coal deposits.

411
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:06,340
By using computers, we enter the information that we have from the aerial photography,

412
00:35:06,760 --> 00:35:13,640
and then the computer will calculate the volume of material that is within a given block that's proposed for mining.

413
00:35:13,880 --> 00:35:19,560
And that will tell us exactly how many yards of overburden we have to remove to get to the coal seam.

414
00:35:20,760 --> 00:35:24,940
Once the coal is extracted, it needs to go through the preparation process.

415
00:35:26,080 --> 00:35:29,800
Rocks and other impurities need to be filtered out through various cleaning procedures.

416
00:35:30,820 --> 00:35:35,820
The coal also needs to be crushed, sized and blended.

417
00:35:35,821 --> 00:35:42,820
Large pieces of coal are broken up into smaller pieces in machines called breakers and crushers.

418
00:35:42,821 --> 00:35:50,820
And the coal is crushed to a size that most utilities utilize and that's about two and a half inches or smaller.

419
00:35:50,821 --> 00:35:54,820
This size burns most efficiently in most power plants.

420
00:35:54,821 --> 00:35:59,820
The processing of coal involves an intricate network of electric motors.

421
00:35:59,821 --> 00:36:05,180
high-capacity belts, and sophisticated sensors, which are all linked to computers.

422
00:36:06,180 --> 00:36:10,000
After it has been washed, the coal is then dried in one of two ways.

423
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:16,120
Either a machine spins the coal dry, or huge vacuum disc filters pull the water out.

424
00:36:18,240 --> 00:36:21,440
The coal is now ready to be delivered to the utility companies.

425
00:36:23,860 --> 00:36:29,380
60% of all coal produced from mines is transported to its destination by railroads.

426
00:36:29,820 --> 00:36:37,860
A large portion is transported in unit trains, 100 or more cars that can be loaded and unloaded automatically.

427
00:36:40,340 --> 00:36:50,000
The coal is conveyed to the loadout silos and it's flood loaded into trains at the rate of about 7,000 tons per hour.

428
00:36:50,480 --> 00:36:55,420
So a unit train that carries about 14,000 tons can be loaded in about two hours.

429
00:36:55,421 --> 00:37:02,740
14,000 tons of coal is enough to power a city of 1.3 million for one day.

430
00:37:04,300 --> 00:37:06,960
Another method of transporting coal is by barges.

431
00:37:07,860 --> 00:37:13,080
Smaller amounts of coal are moved by truck or by conveyor directly from a mine to a power plant.

432
00:37:13,940 --> 00:37:16,920
I've seen so many changes just in the last 10 years.

433
00:37:17,020 --> 00:37:20,680
I never thought we'd be using explosives to actually move the overburden.

434
00:37:21,180 --> 00:37:23,760
I never thought we'd see 400-ton trucks.

435
00:37:23,761 --> 00:37:33,880
I have no idea what the new technologies will be, but I'm sure we will be awed probably by the new technologies that will come out just in the next 10 years.

436
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:40,720
Next, while extraordinary advances in coal mining have led to safer working conditions and increased productivity,

437
00:37:41,460 --> 00:37:44,260
the industry faces a much more daunting challenge.

438
00:37:45,440 --> 00:37:47,740
Now, it has to clean up its act.

439
00:37:47,741 --> 00:37:57,140
With today's state-of-the-art mining machines, a coal miner can produce an average of 52 tons of coal per day.

440
00:37:58,060 --> 00:38:02,020
In 1900, production rates were 2.4 tons per day.

441
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:07,460
Coal Mines will return on Modern Marvels.

442
00:38:10,200 --> 00:38:13,520
We now return to Coal Mines on Modern Marvels.

443
00:38:13,521 --> 00:38:19,760
Coal has always had the reputation of being a dirty fuel.

444
00:38:20,940 --> 00:38:26,680
But the industry has developed new technology that can reduce the four pollutants naturally found in coal.

445
00:38:27,780 --> 00:38:30,300
Sulfur, nitrogen, carbon, and mercury.

446
00:38:31,500 --> 00:38:39,460
The sulfur found in coal combines with oxygen when the coal is burned and produces sulfur dioxides, a major source of air pollution.

447
00:38:39,461 --> 00:38:45,100
acid rain is believed to be another harmful effect of burning coal

448
00:38:45,101 --> 00:38:49,540
the sulfur and nitrogen released into the air when the coal is burned

449
00:38:49,541 --> 00:38:53,440
can combine with water vapor and form droplets that fall to earth

450
00:38:53,441 --> 00:38:56,320
as weak forms of sulfuric and nitric acid

451
00:38:56,321 --> 00:39:00,260
but as a result of the new clean coal technologies

452
00:39:00,261 --> 00:39:04,520
95% of the sulfur and nitrogen in coal can be filtered out

453
00:39:04,521 --> 00:39:06,400
before they can escape into the atmosphere

454
00:39:06,401 --> 00:39:13,400
The Clean Air Act of 1970 provided standards for utilities to meet and essentially required

455
00:39:13,401 --> 00:39:19,280
us to develop many new technologies to better combust the coal.

456
00:39:19,281 --> 00:39:25,440
And so that act was a technology forcing act and we're burning coal much cleaner today

457
00:39:25,441 --> 00:39:29,260
than we were prior to that law being passed.

458
00:39:29,261 --> 00:39:34,840
One method the utility companies initiated was the use of flue gas desulfurization units,

459
00:39:34,841 --> 00:39:39,700
called scrubbers. These scrubbers remove most of the sulfur oxides from the stream

460
00:39:39,701 --> 00:39:45,580
of gases produced by coal combustion before they go up the smokestacks. The flue

461
00:39:45,581 --> 00:39:51,460
gas is sprayed with an alkaline agent such as lime or limestone. The chemical

462
00:39:51,461 --> 00:39:56,720
reaction forms calcium sulfate which is then removed and discarded.

463
00:39:56,721 --> 00:40:01,360
Scrubbers are applied to some power plants and they can reduce emissions of

464
00:40:01,361 --> 00:40:07,360
sulfur dioxide by as much as 90 percent, but the problem is that there's hundreds of plants

465
00:40:07,361 --> 00:40:12,860
that aren't using a full suite of state-of-the-art control technologies, and they're emitting

466
00:40:12,861 --> 00:40:17,880
pollution at four to ten times the rate that would be allowed for a new plant that was

467
00:40:17,881 --> 00:40:18,740
being built today.

468
00:40:20,100 --> 00:40:26,180
Another available but not fully utilized new technology is the precipitator, which removes

469
00:40:26,181 --> 00:40:30,160
fly ash, the dark sooty material that results from burning coal.

470
00:40:30,161 --> 00:40:37,160
Precipitators are filters that electrically charge particles to trap and prevent ash from going up the smokestacks.

471
00:40:37,161 --> 00:40:43,160
These precipitators can eliminate more than 90% of the polluting elements.

472
00:40:43,161 --> 00:40:54,160
A pre-combustion method that can reduce sulfur content from coal by as much as 30% involves simply washing the coal after it is mined,

473
00:40:54,161 --> 00:41:00,080
the waste material is stored and the water is recycled for future use by power plants.

474
00:41:02,120 --> 00:41:07,580
Sulfur emissions are also being reduced by the increased use of coal found in mines that are naturally low in sulfur.

475
00:41:09,120 --> 00:41:16,520
The coal in Wyoming's Powder River Basin, for example, is ten times lower in sulfur than most coal from the Appalachian regions.

476
00:41:16,521 --> 00:41:25,200
using low sulfur coal from the west is only part of a comprehensive set of actions that are needed

477
00:41:25,201 --> 00:41:31,980
to clean up air pollution from coal combustion one of the reasons that we have been thrust to

478
00:41:31,981 --> 00:41:37,720
the forefront of coal production in this country is our low sulfur coal but we also don't have to

479
00:41:37,721 --> 00:41:44,500
wash it so many eastern and midwestern operations will have wash plants we don't we don't have wash

480
00:41:44,501 --> 00:41:50,305
plants. Another problem associated with coal combustion is global warming.

481
00:41:50,306 --> 00:41:51,560
Coal-fired power

482
00:41:51,561 --> 00:41:55,774
plants are the largest source of carbon dioxide in the United States.

483
00:41:55,775 --> 00:41:57,240
Carbon dioxide is a heat

484
00:41:57,241 --> 00:42:02,580
trapping gas that accumulates in the atmosphere, forming a blanket that prevents heat from escaping.

485
00:42:03,400 --> 00:42:10,520
And that blanket is causing temperatures to rise all around the world and causing our climate to

486
00:42:10,521 --> 00:42:11,221
change.

487
00:42:11,222 --> 00:42:16,140
Clean coal technology will address the issue of global warming by one, burning

488
00:42:16,141 --> 00:42:21,600
the coal more efficiently, and we're also working on technologies that will sequester

489
00:42:21,601 --> 00:42:25,860
the carbon dioxide so that we can burn the coal and not put carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.

490
00:42:27,400 --> 00:42:28,100
The U.S.

491
00:42:28,101 --> 00:42:31,940
is joined with other countries in researching various options for sequestering

492
00:42:31,941 --> 00:42:33,939
or trapping the carbon dioxide.

493
00:42:33,940 --> 00:42:38,000
One possibility involves directly collecting the carbon dioxide

494
00:42:38,001 --> 00:42:44,800
from power plants and pumping it into oil and gas reservoirs or on mineable coal seams.

495
00:42:46,660 --> 00:42:51,880
Electric utility companies have spent $70 billion on coal pollution control devices.

496
00:42:53,120 --> 00:42:59,480
Most recently, they are trying to find better methods of reducing toxic metals emissions, like mercury.

497
00:43:00,480 --> 00:43:03,860
One of the clean coal technologies that we can use is gasification.

498
00:43:03,861 --> 00:43:12,420
And what that means is we can use a chemical process to convert the coal from a solid to a gas.

499
00:43:12,900 --> 00:43:17,640
It can be burned in a combined cycle process, so it can be burned at greater efficiencies,

500
00:43:18,160 --> 00:43:24,740
so the source in the future of really clean power, reliable power, will be coal converted to gas.

501
00:43:25,560 --> 00:43:29,680
The gasification of coal seems to be the one thing that everyone agrees upon.

502
00:43:29,681 --> 00:43:36,240
The only technology that I know of that really has promise for making coal truly clean

503
00:43:36,241 --> 00:43:39,920
is integrated gasification combined cycle technology

504
00:43:39,921 --> 00:43:43,560
coupled with carbon dioxide separation and disposal

505
00:43:43,561 --> 00:43:48,080
to eliminate the harmful CO2 emissions that are contributing to global warming.

506
00:43:49,640 --> 00:43:54,580
We still have close to 500,000 square miles of coal under our feet,

507
00:43:54,880 --> 00:43:57,620
enough to last us at least 250 years.

508
00:43:57,621 --> 00:44:04,620
With such a vital resource so abundant, the responsibility that comes along with consuming it grows.

509
00:44:04,621 --> 00:44:10,620
Further technological breakthroughs will be needed to minimize ecological damage.

510
00:44:10,621 --> 00:44:17,620
Hopefully, the industry pushed by government, science and environmental watchdogs will find them.

